1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,210
What if the fate of the world rested in

2
00:00:02,210 --> 00:00:04,300
the hands of just one man?

3
00:00:04,726 --> 00:00:07,136
On September 26, 1983,

4
00:00:07,581 --> 00:00:09,451
during the height of the Cold War,

5
00:00:09,557 --> 00:00:12,157
Soviet officer Stanislav Petrov was on duty

6
00:00:12,157 --> 00:00:15,007
at a secret early-warning command center.

7
00:00:15,314 --> 00:00:16,884
Suddenly, alarms erupted.

8
00:00:17,494 --> 00:00:19,974
The Soviet satellite system reported that

9
00:00:19,974 --> 00:00:22,294
American nuclear missiles were heading toward

10
00:00:22,294 --> 00:00:23,304
the Soviet Union.

11
00:00:23,654 --> 00:00:26,114
According to procedure, Petrov had to report

12
00:00:26,114 --> 00:00:28,124
the warning immediately to his superiors.

13
00:00:28,739 --> 00:00:30,659
A false decision could have pushed the world

14
00:00:30,659 --> 00:00:32,089
toward nuclear catastrophe.

15
00:00:32,838 --> 00:00:34,428
But something didn't feel right.

16
00:00:34,798 --> 00:00:37,388
The system initially detected just one launch,

17
00:00:37,638 --> 00:00:38,908
followed by four more.

18
00:00:39,183 --> 00:00:40,833
Petrov questioned the warning.

19
00:00:41,072 --> 00:00:42,512
A real American first strike,

20
00:00:42,512 --> 00:00:44,632
he reasoned, would hardly begin with only

21
00:00:44,632 --> 00:00:45,902
five missiles.

22
00:00:46,470 --> 00:00:48,790
He decided the alert was probably a false

23
00:00:48,790 --> 00:00:50,880
alarm and reported it that way.

24
00:00:51,301 --> 00:00:52,091
Minutes passed.

25
00:00:52,636 --> 00:00:53,786
No missiles arrived.

26
00:00:54,396 --> 00:00:56,496
Investigators later discovered that sunlight

27
00:00:56,496 --> 00:00:59,436
reflecting off high-altitude clouds had

28
00:00:59,436 --> 00:01:01,426
fooled the Soviet satellite system.

29
00:01:01,764 --> 00:01:04,344
Petrov's judgment helped prevent a dangerous

30
00:01:04,344 --> 00:01:07,164
miscalculation during one of the Cold War's

31
00:01:07,164 --> 00:01:08,094
tensest moments.

32
00:01:08,643 --> 00:01:10,693
He was not a man who launched a weapon.

33
00:01:10,882 --> 00:01:12,902
He was the man who refused to mistake a

34
00:01:12,902 --> 00:01:14,032
warning for certainty.

35
00:01:14,622 --> 00:01:16,562
Sometimes, the bravest decision is knowing

36
00:01:16,562 --> 00:01:18,092
when not to act.

